  abstract = {Introduction We are reporting the results over a 20yr period of simultaneous pancreas-kidney transplants in patients with end-stage renal disease and diabetes mellitus. The outcomes of the transplants, performed between 1989 and 2008, are stratified by pretransplant c-peptide value. Methods One hundred and seventy-three patients with end-stage renal disease due to diabetes, and were stratified according to undetectable c-peptide (x 0.8ng/mL) levels. Results Patients with detectable c-peptide (x{$>$}0.8ng/mL) were the oldest at diabetes diagnosis (24.2 vs. 15.4yr, p 0.8ng/mL) had better graft survival than those with an undetectable c-peptide level (x{$<$}0.8ng/mL), p=0.064; while those with undetectable levels, had better patient survival than those with detectable c-peptide levels (p=0.019). Conclusion Despite the differences between groups by BMI, age of onset of insulin use, and age at transplant, there was a difference in patient but not graft survival within the 20yr follow-up period.},
